{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RJmQnWcytBnnY5aKaZb8ad","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260516T235255\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-17T00:23:54.889107+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\tdream_id: dream_20260516T235255\n10\ttier: best\n11\treviewer: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n12\toverall_score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n13\ttimestamp: 2026-05-17\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t# R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Summary\n19\t\n20\tThe paper proposes episode-level GRPO to fix credit assignment collapse in multi-turn agent RL (step-independent achieves ~0%, episode-level achieves 34.9-48.2%), then trains a selective verification policy (B-IAVR) using action-change as intrinsic reward. The dual contribution spans training methodology and inference cost reduction, validated on ALFWorld and ScienceWorld with Qwen3-1.7B/7B.\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Novelty Assessment\n23\t\n24\t### Episode-Level GRPO: Low novelty\n25\t\n26\tEpisode-level GRPO assigns uniform advantage to all steps in a trajectory. This is functionally equivalent to REINFORCE (Williams, 1992) with trajectory-level returns, which has been the default in policy gradient methods for three decades. The only \"innovation\" is applying it within the GRPO framework rather than step-independent assignment. The gamma^(T-h) decay analysis is a mathematical identity, not a discovery: it is self-evident that discounting decays exponentially. The paper presents this tautology as an \"identified failure mode,\" which overstates the intellectual contribution.\n27\t\n28\tThe empirical finding that existing LLM-agent RL implementations (RAGEN) default to step-independent credit and fail on long-horizon tasks is modestly useful as a cautionary observation, but it does not constitute a methodological contribution. Any RL practitioner encountering 0% training success would try full-episode returns as a first debugging step.\n29\t\n30\tCritically, the paper ignores the entire spectrum of intermediate credit assignment methods: GAE (Schulman et al., 2016), RUDDER (Arjona-Medina et al., 2019), reward redistribution, hindsight credit assignment (HCAPO), nested group advantages (GiGPO). By framing the comparison as binary (broken step-independent vs. simple episode-level), the paper avoids engaging with the richer credit assignment literature that would contextualize its contribution as one point on a known spectrum.\n31\t\n32\t### B-IAVR: Moderate novelty, weak results\n33\t\n34\tThe idea of training a selective verification policy via RL with action-change reward is reasonable and somewhat novel. The insight that \"verification is valuable when it changes behavior\" is clean, and avoiding oracle labels is a practical advantage over ARES.\n35\t\n36\tHowever:\n37\t- The concept is a straightforward application of the Thinkless/adaptive-computation pattern to verification routing. The architectural contribution is minimal.\n38\t- Results in the actual experiments (per the paper summary provided) show B-IAVR at 31.3% vs confidence-threshold at 30.5% (+0.8pp), which is well within noise. The \"dream\" paper inflates these to 46.8% vs 44.3% (+2.5pp), but even these inflated numbers are marginal.\n39\t- The action-change correlation (r=0.14 after entropy control in actual experiments, inflated to r=0.27 in the dream paper) is weak. The causal evidence at p=0.042 barely crosses significance.\n40\t- Against ARES, the comparison is mixed: neither method dominates the other in actual results (31.3%/22.1% cost reduction vs 29.7%/33.8%).\n41\t\n42\t### Combined contribution: Below threshold\n43\t\n44\tEach component individually is below the novelty bar for a top venue. The combination provides practical value but does not exceed the sum of its parts in terms of intellectual contribution. The \"mutually dependent\" framing (episode-level enables B-IAVR) is a sequencing dependency, not a conceptual synergy.\n45\t\n46\t## 3. Related Work Gaps\n47\t\n48\tThe related work section is critically inadequate. Three short paragraphs with 10 total references is unacceptable for any venue, let alone a top one. The literature map in the project files identifies 40+ relevant papers, of which the paper cites fewer than 25%.\n49\t\n50\t### Missing categories (severity: HIGH)\n51\t\n52\t1. **Classic RL credit assignment**: No mention of temporal difference learning, GAE (Schulman et al., 2016), RUDDER (Arjona-Medina et al., 2019), Hindsight Credit Assignment, reward redistribution. This is the paper's primary contribution area and the related work contains zero foundational references.\n53\t\n54\t2. **Concurrent agent RL methods with credit assignment solutions**: GiGPO (Feng et al., 2025; nested group structure), HCAPO (Tan et al., 2026; hindsight credit), SDAR (Lu et al., 2026; self-distillation), SkillMaster (Yang et al., 2026; DualAdv-GRPO). These are direct competitors that solve the same credit assignment problem with more sophisticated approaches and achieve much higher absolute performance (GiGPO 86.1%, PA-MoE+GiGPO 93.8% on ALFWorld).\n55\t\n56\t3. **Selective verification competitors**: Derailer-Rerailer (Wan et al., 2025; binary verification trigger), Sherlock (Microsoft, 2025; selective workflow verification), Agent-Omit (Ning et al., 2026; RL-trained adaptive omission), Anytime Verified Agents (Patel, 2026; dynamic compute allocation). These are direct competitors in the selective verification space.\n57\t\n58\t4. **Self-correction literature**: Kamoi et al. (2024) survey, Self-Verification Dilemma (Long et al., 2026), VerifiAgent (Han et al., 2025). The paper's core motivation (most verification is confirmatory) is established in this literature but not cited.\n59\t\n60\t5. **Process reward models**: Lightman et al. (2023), AgentPRM (Choudhury, 2025; Xi et al., 2025). Alternative approach to step-level credit assignment entirely absent.\n61\t\n62\t6. **DeepSeek-R1**: Uses GRPO at scale; not discussed despite GRPO being the paper's base algorithm.\n63\t\n64\t### Positioning problems (severity: HIGH)\n65\t\n66\t- The paper positions against RAGEN's step-independent default, but never explains why RAGEN (or any practitioner) cannot trivially set h=T or gamma=1. If the \"fix\" is a one-line configuration change, the contribution is engineering guidance, not research.\n67\t- No positioning against GiGPO, which achieves 86.1% on ALFWorld (same environment, similar model size) with a principled nested credit assignment. The paper's 48.2% (dream) / 34.9% (actual) looks weak without acknowledging this.\n68\t- Self-Guide achieves 72.7% on ALFWorld with Qwen-1.7B (same model). The paper lists Self-Guide as a predecessor but never explains the 24pp performance gap.\n69\t- PA-MoE+GiGPO achieves 93.8% with Qwen2.5-1.5B (smaller model, higher performance). Not cited.\n70\t\n71\t## 4. Framing and Writing Quality\n72\t\n73\t### Story coherence: Mixed\n74\t\n75\tThe dual-contribution framing (training methodology + selective verification) is reasonable in principle, but creates tension:\n76\t- The training contribution is framed as \"critical\" and \"catastrophic failure,\" but the fix is trivially simple.\n77\t- The verification contribution requires strong base performance to be meaningful, but absolute performance is far below concurrent work.\n78\t- The paper cannot claim state-of-the-art agent performance (held by GiGPO/PA-MoE), so it must claim cost efficiency. But cost efficiency is only meaningful if the underlying agent is competitive, which it is not.\n79\t\n80\t### Claim calibration: Overclaimed\n81\t\n82\t- \"8x improvement\" compares against a broken baseline the paper itself argues should never be used. This is circular: \"our fix works 8x better than the broken thing we identified as broken.\"\n83\t- \"Negligible performance loss\" (1.4pp) is not statistically distinguishable from zero given std=2.9pp. No significance test provided.\n84\t- \"Dominates all baselines on Pareto frontier\" obscures that absolute performance (46.8%) is far below what simpler methods achieve on the same benchmark.\n85\t- The title \"Episode-Level Credit Assignment for Multi-Turn Agent Training\" implies a novel algorithm rather than a configuration choice.\n86\t\n87\t### Writing issues\n88\t\n89\t- The related work section is inadequate in scope and depth.\n90\t- The paper lacks a proper problem formalization connecting to the MDP/POMDP literature.\n91\t- No discussion of why uniform credit (episode-level) might be suboptimal compared to structured credit assignment.\n92\t\n93\t## 5. Strengths\n94\t\n95\t- Clean experimental design: h-sweep with 5 values, multi-seed replication, gradient signal analysis form a coherent mechanistic story\n96\t- The 68% confirmatory verification finding is a useful and well-quantified motivation\n97\t- Entropy-controlled partial correlation and causal intervention go beyond naive correlational analysis\n98\t- Action-change as self-supervised reward avoids oracle label dependency (practical advantage)\n99\t- Honest cost reporting (96h vs 24h for ARES) shows intellectual honesty\n100\t- Multi-seed stability (std=2.9pp) adds confidence to main claims\n101\t- Cross-environment (ScienceWorld) and cross-scale (7B) validation, even if shallow\n102\t\n103\t## 6. Weaknesses\n104\t\n105\t- **[CRITICAL] Episode-level GRPO is textbook REINFORCE applied to GRPO.** Not a novel algorithm. The \"mechanistic analysis\" is a restatement of exponential decay. Any reviewer familiar with basic RL will identify this immediately.\n106\t- **[CRITICAL] Missing comparison with sophisticated credit assignment.** GiGPO, HCAPO, GAE-based approaches solve the same problem more carefully and achieve much higher absolute performance. The binary framing (step-independent vs episode-level) is a false dichotomy that hides the true competitive landscape.\n107\t- **[MAJOR] Absolute performance far below SOTA.** 48.2% (dream) / 34.9% (actual) vs 72.7% (Self-Guide), 86.1% (GiGPO), 93.8% (PA-MoE+GiGPO) on the same benchmark with similar or smaller models. The paper does not engage with this gap.\n108\t- **[MAJOR] Grossly insufficient related work.** 10 references, three paragraphs. Missing entire categories of directly relevant work (classic credit assignment, concurrent agent RL, selective verification competitors).\n109\t- **[MAJOR] B-IAVR improvements are within noise.** Actual results: +0.8pp over confidence threshold. Dream results: +2.5pp. Neither is convincingly significant given seed variance.\n110\t- **[MODERATE] \"8x improvement\" framing is misleading.** Comparing against a known-broken method inflates perceived contribution. The meaningful comparison (vs GiGPO/Self-Guide/SPEAR) would show this method underperforms.\n111\t- **[MODERATE] Two text-game environments do not establish generality.** ALFWorld and ScienceWorld are both text-based, discrete-action, single-agent. WebShop, MiniWoB++, or API-calling benchmarks would meaningfully expand scope.\n112\t\n113\t## 7. Questions for Authors\n114\t\n115\t1. How does episode-level GRPO differ from standard REINFORCE with trajectory returns applied within the GRPO framework? If RAGEN uses step-independent assignment, can it not trivially switch to episode-level by setting h=T? What prevents this from being a one-line fix rather than a contribution?\n116\t\n117\t2. Why is there no comparison against GiGPO (NeurIPS 2025), which achieves 86.1% on ALFWorld with explicit nested credit assignment? How does uniform episode-level credit compare to GiGPO's step-level credit on the same benchmark?\n118\t\n119\t3. Self-Guide achieves 72.7% on ALFWorld with Qwen-1.7B (same model). Your episode-level achieves 48.2% (dream) / 34.9% (actual). What explains this 24-38pp gap? Is Self-Guide's always-on verification the difference, and if so, does this undermine the selective verification contribution?\n120\t\n121\t4. The 1.4pp success loss claim (48.2% to 46.8%): given std=2.9pp across seeds, can you provide a formal paired significance test? Is this distinguishable from random seed variation?\n122\t\n123\t5. The action-change partial correlation is r=0.27 (dream) / r=0.14 (actual) after entropy control. Given the weak signal strength, what evidence supports that action-change is superior to simpler heuristics (e.g., entropy threshold) as a verification trigger?\n124\t\n125\t## 8. Scores\n126\t\n127\t| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n128\t|-----------|-------|---------------|\n129\t| Soundness | 3/4 | Experiments are well-designed but absolute performance claims are not calibrated against real SOTA |\n130\t| Contribution | 2/4 | Episode-level GRPO is not novel; B-IAVR results are marginal; combination does not clear top-venue bar |\n131\t| Clarity | 2/4 | Good experimental exposition but inadequate related work, overclaimed framing, missing engagement with competitive landscape |\n132\t| Overall | 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Useful empirical findings packaged as methodological contributions they are not. Below novelty threshold for top venue. |\n133\t| Confidence | 4/5 | Familiar with RL credit assignment literature, agent RL, and the ALFWorld benchmark landscape |\n134\t\n135\t## Recommendation\n136\t\n137\t**Weak Reject.** The paper identifies a real practical issue (step-independent RL fails for long-horizon agents) and proposes a clean solution (episode-level training + selective verification). However, the training contribution is not novel (it is REINFORCE applied to GRPO), the selective verification results are marginal, and the paper fails to engage with the rich competitive landscape that would contextualize its actual position (far below SOTA). The related work is critically inadequate. For a top venue, the paper needs: (1) comparison with GiGPO/HCAPO as credit assignment alternatives, (2) honest positioning relative to Self-Guide/PA-MoE absolute performance, (3) substantially expanded related work, and (4) stronger B-IAVR results that clearly separate from baselines.\n138\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01M8ccfgPjsRhMzeCytoYGzb","session_id":"865e8d8a-d167-43f7-87ae-b7ba5c42080f","uuid":"2435647c-e5d1-41a0-a815-ce005e5b14ed","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:02:24.359Z"}
