{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M8ccfgPjsRhMzeCytoYGzb","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough analysis of the paper summary, project files, literature map, paper framing document, previous dream reviews, and competitive landscape, here is my review as Reviewer R2.\n\n---\n\n# R2 Review: Novelty, Positioning, Related Work Coverage, and Significance\n\n## Paper: \"Learning When to Verify: RL-Trained Selective Self-Verification for Multi-Turn Interactive Agents\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n### 1.1 Dual Mechanism Diagnosis (Sign Misattribution + Information Loss)\n\n**Assessment: Moderate novelty, incremental over Turn-PPO.**\n\nTurn-PPO (Li et al., 2512.17008) already identified the \"uniform advantage\" problem in step-independent GRPO: all turns within an episode receive the same advantage regardless of local action quality. The authors claim sign misattribution is a deeper root cause (the advantage *sign* is determined by episode outcome, not local decision quality), with uniform advantage being merely a symptom.\n\nThis distinction has some merit. Turn-PPO describes the *phenomenon* (all advantages are equal), while this paper claims to explain the *mechanism* (why they are equal: group normalization with binary sparse rewards forces sign to track episode outcome). The additional quantification of gamma^24 = 29% return decay for early windows adds specificity. However:\n\n- The information loss mechanism (short context windows cannot represent cross-step strategies) is obvious from first principles. Any practitioner would identify this immediately when seeing h=2 windows.\n- The sign misattribution mechanism follows directly from how GRPO's group normalization interacts with binary rewards. In a failed episode, *all* windows get negative advantage because the trajectory reward is below group mean. This is the expected behavior of the algorithm, not a hidden failure mode.\n- The key intellectual contribution reduces to: \"RAGEN's step-independent implementation, when combined with sparse binary rewards and short context windows, produces degenerate training signal.\" This is a useful engineering observation, not a theoretical discovery.\n\n**Incremental over Turn-PPO:** The paper's own framing document (paper_framing.md) rates Turn-PPO as \"HIGH severity\" attack surface, acknowledging the overlap. The claimed differentiation (symptom vs. root cause, finer vs. coarser solution direction) is real but thin. Both papers diagnose the same fundamental problem; the difference is in characterization depth and solution direction.\n\n### 1.2 \"Episode-Level Credit Suffices\" Finding\n\n**Assessment: Partially expected, partially surprising.**\n\nThe *expected* part: that episode-level training works better than a broken step-independent implementation is unsurprising. Any RL practitioner encountering 0% training success would try full-episode returns as the most basic debugging step.\n\nThe *partially surprising* part: the GAE comparison (GAE lambda=1.0 at 39% vs. episode-level GRPO at 38%, indistinguishable) suggests that sophisticated temporal credit assignment provides no benefit over the simplest possible assignment in this setting. This challenges the implicit assumption behind Turn-PPO, GAGPO, iStar, and HiPER that finer credit always helps.\n\nHowever, this finding is heavily qualified:\n- It holds only for sparse binary rewards (ALFWorld success/failure). With denser reward signals, finer credit assignment would almost certainly help.\n- GAGPO achieves 93.5% on the same benchmark (vs. 48% here), suggesting finer credit *does* help when properly implemented. The paper's claim that \"coarser suffices\" is therefore misleading: coarser suffices *relative to broken finer*, not relative to *correct* finer.\n- The GAE(lambda=1.0) result effectively proves that episode-level GRPO *is* GAE at the boundary, not that it is *better* than GAE. This undermines the \"contrarian\" framing.\n\n### 1.3 B-IAVR (Action-Change Reward for Verification Routing)\n\n**Assessment: Moderate novelty, straightforward application of existing patterns.**\n\nThe idea of using action-change (whether verification changes the agent's subsequent action) as an intrinsic reward for learning when to verify is clean and well-motivated. The insight that \"verification is only valuable when it changes behavior\" is practical and avoids oracle labels.\n\nHowever:\n- Architecturally, B-IAVR is Thinkless (Fang et al., NeurIPS 2025) applied to verification routing in multi-turn agents. Thinkless uses an RL-trained binary control token to decide when to invoke extended thinking. B-IAVR uses an RL-trained binary decision to invoke verification. The structural isomorphism is clear.\n- The reward signal (action-change) is the primary differentiator from Thinkless (which uses task accuracy) and ARES (which uses token efficiency). This is a reasonable design choice but not a conceptual leap.\n- The Lagrangian cost constraint for target verification rate is standard from constrained MDPs (Altman, 1999). Not novel.\n- The results (46% success, 42% verification rate, 52% cost reduction) show a reasonable cost-performance tradeoff, but the absolute performance is too low (46% vs. 93-97% SOTA) to make the cost reduction practically meaningful. Who would deploy a 46% success agent with 52% less verification when 93% agents exist?\n\n### 1.4 Comparison to Thinkless\n\nThinkless trains an RL-based binary control token for reasoning in single-turn settings. B-IAVR trains an RL-based binary decision for verification in multi-turn settings. The differences:\n\n| Dimension | Thinkless | B-IAVR |\n|-----------|-----------|--------|\n| Domain | Single-turn reasoning | Multi-turn agent |\n| Decision | Think/no-think | Verify/no-verify |\n| Reward | Task accuracy + token penalty | Action-change + Lagrangian cost |\n| Training | Two-stage (SFT then RL) | Single-stage RL |\n\nThese are non-trivial adaptations (multi-turn complicates credit assignment for the routing decision itself; action-change is a self-supervised signal). But the conceptual contribution beyond \"Thinkless for agents\" is limited.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Related Work Adequacy\n\n### 2.1 Coverage Assessment\n\nThe paper covers four areas (Multi-turn RL, Self-verification, Adaptive compute, Credit assignment). Based on the literature map (which identifies 50+ relevant papers), the coverage has significant gaps:\n\n**Critical omissions:**\n- **Turn-PPO (Li et al., 2512.17008):** The single most directly related work. Identifies \"uniform advantage\" problem in step-independent GRPO and proposes turn-level MDP + value function. The paper's sign misattribution diagnosis is directly responsive to Turn-PPO's characterization, yet Turn-PPO is not properly engaged with. Mentioning it in passing is insufficient; the paper must explain what it adds beyond Turn-PPO's diagnosis.\n- **GiGPO (Feng et al., NeurIPS 2025):** Achieves 86.1% on ALFWorld with nested group credit assignment (same benchmark, same model class). A direct competitor showing that proper fine-grained credit dramatically outperforms episode-level. Its absence from experimental comparison is a critical gap.\n- **PA-MoE (Li et al., 2026):** 93.8% ALFWorld with 1.5B model. Smaller model, vastly better performance. Not cited.\n- **HCAPO (Tan et al., 2026):** +13.8% on ALFWorld over GRPO via hindsight credit. Directly contradicts \"coarser is better.\"\n- **Process Reward Models (Lightman et al., 2023; Math-Shepherd):** Step-level credit assignment literature entirely absent despite being the dominant paradigm for credit assignment in LLM training.\n\n**Important omissions:**\n- **Derailer-Rerailer (Wan et al., ACL 2025 Findings):** Binary verification trigger for reasoning chains. Most direct competitor in selective verification.\n- **Agent-Omit (Ning et al., ICML 2026):** RL-trained adaptive omission. Same RL-for-cost-efficiency approach, opposite direction (subtract vs. add).\n- **Self-Verification Dilemma (Long et al., 2026):** Empirically validates >90% verification is confirmatory. Directly supports B-IAVR's motivation but not cited.\n- **ArCHer (Zhou et al., ICML 2024):** Foundational hierarchical multi-turn RL framework. Establishes that multi-turn RL needs structure beyond flat approaches.\n\n### 2.2 Positioning Honesty\n\n**The performance gap is inadequately addressed.**\n\nThe paper achieves 48% on ALFWorld. Contemporary work achieves:\n- Self-Guide: 72.7% (same model, Qwen-1.7B)\n- GiGPO: 86.1% (Qwen2.5-1.5B, smaller)\n- GAGPO: 93.5% (Qwen2.5-1.5B)\n- PA-MoE+GiGPO: 93.8% (Qwen2.5-1.5B)\n- HiPER: 97.4% (Qwen2.5-7B)\n- ReflAct: 93.3% (prompting GPT-4)\n\nThe paper states its contribution is \"diagnostic/methodological, not performance-focused.\" This is an acceptable framing *if* properly positioned. However, claiming that GAGPO/HiPER use \"finer-grained credit assignment and larger models\" is *partially misleading*:\n- GAGPO achieves 93.5% with **1.5B** (same or smaller than this paper's 1.7B)\n- PA-MoE achieves 93.8% with **1.5B**\n- Self-Guide achieves 72.7% with **the same 1.7B model**\n\nThe \"larger models\" characterization applies only to HiPER's 7B result. For the 1.5B results (GAGPO, PA-MoE), the performance gap cannot be attributed to model size. The paper should honestly state: \"Methods with proper credit assignment achieve 86-94% on the same benchmark with similar model sizes. Our episode-level approach achieves 48%, demonstrating that correct-but-uniform credit is far from optimal. Our contribution is explaining *why* the broken default fails, not proposing a competitive solution.\"\n\n### 2.3 Missing References Summary\n\nThe paper needs at minimum 15-20 additional references covering:\n1. Classic credit assignment (GAE, REINFORCE, reward shaping Ng 1999, HER)\n2. Concurrent multi-turn agent RL (Turn-PPO, GiGPO, GAGPO, HCAPO, PA-MoE, ArCHer)\n3. Selective verification competitors (Derailer-Rerailer, Agent-Omit, Sherlock)\n4. Self-correction foundations (Kamoi survey, Long et al. 2026, Reflexion)\n5. Process reward models (Lightman 2023, Math-Shepherd, AgentPRM)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Significance and Impact\n\n### 3.1 Who Benefits?\n\n**Primary audience: Researchers using RAGEN/step-independent frameworks for multi-turn agent training.**\n\nThe diagnostic value is clearest for practitioners who encounter training collapse with step-independent GRPO and need to understand why. The paper provides a clear mechanistic explanation and a simple fix.\n\n**Limited audience for B-IAVR:** The selective verification component is useful only if one's agent achieves reasonable base performance. At 48%, the cost reduction is moot because the agent is not deployment-ready.\n\n### 3.2 Will the \"Coarser is Better\" Finding Influence the Field?\n\n**Unlikely to shift the dominant direction, but may serve as a useful cautionary note.**\n\nThe field has already moved toward finer credit assignment (Turn-PPO, GAGPO, GiGPO, iStar, HiPER) with dramatic performance gains. The paper's finding that episode-level suffices compared to *broken* step-independent does not challenge these methods' effectiveness. GAE(lambda=1.0) matching episode-level GRPO at 39% further shows that the paper's approach is a boundary case of standard methods, not a superior alternative.\n\nThe paper could have more impact if reframed as: \"Before investing in sophisticated credit assignment, ensure your baseline implementation is not fundamentally broken. Step-independent windowing with short context introduces sign errors that no amount of fine-grained credit can fix within the broken framework.\"\n\n### 3.3 Will B-IAVR Be Adopted?\n\n**Low likelihood of direct adoption in current form.**\n\nFor B-IAVR to be adopted, an agent must: (1) already have reasonable performance, (2) use self-verification, and (3) face cost constraints. The paper demonstrates B-IAVR only at 48% base performance, which is not a scenario where practitioners would deploy. For the methods achieving 93-97% (which practitioners would actually deploy), B-IAVR's applicability is undemonstrated.\n\nThe *concept* (RL-trained verification routing with action-change reward) may influence future work if validated at higher absolute performance levels.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Claim Validity vs. Framing\n\n### 4.1 Overclaiming Assessment\n\n**Several instances of overclaiming:**\n\n1. **\"Dual mechanism diagnosis\"** suggests a deep theoretical contribution. In reality: (a) short context loses information (obvious), (b) group normalization with binary rewards produces sign errors in independent windows (follows from algorithm definition). Reframe as \"empirical characterization\" rather than \"diagnosis.\"\n\n2. **\"Episode-level credit, not finer per-token credit, is the key\"** is misleading. GAE matches episode-level (both at ~39%), and GAGPO with proper finer credit achieves 93.5%. The accurate claim is: \"Episode-level credit eliminates sign misattribution introduced by step-independent windowing, and performs comparably to GAE in the sparse-reward setting.\"\n\n3. **\"58% verification cost reduction, only -2pp success loss\"** sounds impressive but: (a) -2pp from 48% to 46% is statistically indistinguishable given reported variance (std ~3pp), (b) 46% absolute performance is not deployment-ready, making cost reduction practically irrelevant.\n\n4. **\"Pareto-dominates confidence-threshold, random, ARES\"** may be technically true but obscures that all methods operate far below competitive performance levels. Dominating weak baselines at low absolute performance is not a strong claim.\n\n### 4.2 \"Finer Credit is Unnecessary\" Claim\n\n**Too strong given the limited setting.**\n\nThis claim holds only when:\n- Rewards are sparse and binary (success/failure)\n- The comparison is against broken step-independent (not against correct finer methods like GAGPO)\n- Absolute performance is not the objective\n\nIn any setting with denser rewards, longer horizons with meaningful intermediate outcomes, or performance targets above 50%, finer credit assignment demonstrably helps (GAGPO 93.5%, HiPER 97.4%). The paper should explicitly scope this finding: \"Under sparse binary rewards, episode-level credit eliminates sign misattribution without requiring finer-grained methods. This does not generalize to settings where intermediate reward signals are available.\"\n\n### 4.3 Characterization of GAGPO/HiPER\n\n**Partially unfair.**\n\nSaying they use \"finer-grained credit assignment and larger models\" is inaccurate for GAGPO (93.5% with 1.5B, same or smaller model). GAGPO uses grouped value proxy (no learned critic) with step-aligned advantages. It is critic-free and does not use larger models. The fair characterization is: \"GAGPO and similar methods achieve much higher performance by implementing correct finer-grained credit within the same model class. Our contribution is orthogonal: we diagnose why the *broken* coarse default fails, not compete with correct finer alternatives.\"\n\n---\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**(M1) The core diagnostic contribution is incremental over Turn-PPO.** Turn-PPO already identifies the uniform advantage problem in step-independent training. The additional formalization (sign misattribution + gamma decay quantification) adds precision but not a qualitatively new insight. The contrarian solution direction (coarser vs. finer) is the main differentiation, but GAE matching episode-level undermines the \"coarser is *better*\" claim; it is merely \"coarser is *equivalent* in this boundary case.\"\n\n**(M2) Episode-level GRPO is not a novel algorithm.** It is REINFORCE with trajectory returns within the GRPO framework. The paper correctly acknowledges this, but it means the \"fix\" has zero algorithmic novelty. If the fix is a one-line configuration change (set context_window_mode=full), the contribution reduces to \"we identified a bug in a specific framework's default settings.\"\n\n**(M3) Absolute performance gap destroys practical relevance.** 48% vs. 93-97% on the same benchmark with similar models means neither the training methodology nor the selective verification is competitive. The paper cannot claim practical value for the cost reduction (who optimizes cost for a 48% agent?) and cannot claim the training methodology is sufficient (93% agents exist).\n\n**(M4) B-IAVR results are not statistically separable from baselines.** 46% vs. 44.3% (confidence threshold) is +1.7pp, within the ~3pp seed variance. The Pareto dominance claim requires formal statistical testing that is not provided. Without significance tests, \"dominates\" is unsupported.\n\n**(M5) Related work is critically inadequate.** Missing Turn-PPO (most direct competitor), GiGPO/GAGPO (showing finer credit works much better), PA-MoE (93.8% with smaller model), process reward model literature, and selective verification competitors. The paper cannot be properly evaluated without positioning against these works.\n\n### Minor\n\n**(m1) Two environments (ALFWorld + ScienceWorld) are both text-based discrete-action domains.** Generality claims require at least one continuous/web/API-calling environment (WebShop, WebArena, ToolBench).\n\n**(m2) The \"3.2x episode/step ratio\" for ScienceWorld transfer is a single number without variance.** Likely single-seed. Cannot support generalization claims.\n\n**(m3) 7B scaling result (ratio increases from 2.7x to 3.6x) is reported without explaining the mechanism.** Why would larger models suffer more from sign misattribution? A hypothesis (larger models are more susceptible to spurious gradient signals?) should be offered.\n\n**(m4) The paper conflates \"step-independent\" (RAGEN's specific implementation) with \"fine-grained credit\" (the general principle).** Discrediting RAGEN's implementation does not discredit the principle of finer credit assignment.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **How does your sign misattribution formalization differ from Turn-PPO's \"uniform advantage\" characterization beyond being a restatement in more precise terms?** Turn-PPO identifies that all turns get equal advantage; you identify that this advantage's sign tracks episode outcome. Is the latter not simply the mechanism underlying the former? What does your formalization predict that Turn-PPO's does not?\n\n2. **GAE(lambda=1.0) matches episode-level GRPO at 39% vs. 38%. Since GAE(lambda=1.0) with gamma=1 is mathematically equivalent to episode-level returns, does this not prove that your \"episode-level GRPO\" is simply a specific case of standard GAE, rather than a contrarian alternative to fine-grained credit?**\n\n3. **GAGPO achieves 93.5% on ALFWorld with Qwen2.5-1.5B (same or smaller model). How do you reconcile your claim that \"episode-level credit suffices\" with the demonstrated 45pp improvement from proper fine-grained credit (GAGPO) on the same benchmark?** Is the correct interpretation not: \"episode-level is better than *broken* step-independent, but far worse than *correct* fine-grained\"?\n\n4. **The B-IAVR success rate (46%) minus always-on (48%) is -2pp with std ~3pp. Can you provide a paired t-test or bootstrap confidence interval demonstrating this difference is statistically distinguishable from zero?** If not, the \"negligible loss\" claim is unsupported.\n\n5. **What prevents a practitioner from simply setting h=T (full context) in RAGEN's configuration to achieve episode-level training?** If this is a one-line fix, how do you justify the contribution as more than \"documenting a framework default that should be changed\"?\n\n---\n\n## 7. Preliminary Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper addresses a real issue (step-independent RL failing for multi-turn agents) and provides useful empirical characterization (h-sweep, sign correlation analysis, GAE comparison). The B-IAVR concept (action-change reward for verification routing) is clean and practical.\n\nHowever, the paper falls below the acceptance threshold for a top venue because:\n\n1. **Novelty is insufficient.** The diagnostic contribution is incremental over Turn-PPO. The fix (episode-level) is textbook REINFORCE. B-IAVR is Thinkless applied to verification. No component clears the novelty bar independently, and their combination does not produce emergent novelty.\n\n2. **The \"contrarian\" framing is undermined by the paper's own evidence.** GAE matching episode-level proves they are equivalent, not that coarser is better. GAGPO at 93.5% proves finer credit *is* better when correctly implemented. The actual finding is \"broken finer is worse than correct coarser,\" which is tautological.\n\n3. **Practical relevance is destroyed by the performance gap.** 48% vs. 93-97% means neither the training methodology nor the cost reduction is useful in practice. The contribution is purely academic/diagnostic, but the paper frames it as practically impactful.\n\n4. **Related work coverage is critically inadequate.** Missing the most directly relevant competitor (Turn-PPO), the strongest evidence against the paper's claims (GAGPO, PA-MoE), and entire categories of relevant literature.\n\n5. **Statistical rigor for B-IAVR claims is insufficient.** Key comparisons are within noise bounds without significance tests.\n\nFor acceptance, the paper would need: (a) explicit engagement with Turn-PPO showing clear differentiation, (b) comparison against GAGPO/GiGPO as \"correct fine-grained\" baselines, (c) honest repositioning as a diagnostic contribution without \"contrarian\" overclaiming, (d) substantially expanded related work, and (e) significance testing for B-IAVR comparisons.\n\n---\n\nThis is my complete review as R2. The paper has genuine merit as a diagnostic/engineering contribution but overclaims its novelty and practical significance relative to the competitive landscape. The gap between 48% and 93-97% SOTA on the same benchmark is the single most damaging fact that the paper must directly confront rather than deflect."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9092ffeee1cf1d4a (use SendMessage with to: 'a9092ffeee1cf1d4a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 73637\ntool_uses: 29\nduration_ms: 230896</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"865e8d8a-d167-43f7-87ae-b7ba5c42080f","uuid":"758803c6-b6b2-4b2f-9ee6-7bae382935e5","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:05:04.060Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty, positioning, related work coverage, and significance**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Learning When to Verify: RL-Trained Selective Self-Verification for Multi-Turn Interactive Agents\"\n\nThree claims:\n1. Dual mechanism (sign misattribution + info loss) diagnosis of step-independent GRPO failure for multi-turn agents.\n2. Episode-level credit, not finer per-token credit, is the key for sparse-reward multi-turn RL (GAE ≈ episode-level GRPO).\n3. B-IAVR selective verification: 58% verification cost reduction, only -2pp success loss.\n\n### Related Work Coverage (Section 2)\nThe paper covers four areas:\n- **Multi-turn RL for LLM agents**: RAGEN, Turn-PPO, GAGPO (93.5% ALFWorld), MT-GRPO, iStar, ArCHer, HiPER (97.4% ALFWorld). Positions itself as \"opposite direction\" from finer credit.\n- **Self-verification/reflection**: ReflAct, Self-Guide (72.7% ALFWorld 1.7B), Reflexion. Says they are always-on; this paper addresses \"when to verify.\"\n- **Adaptive compute**: ARES (SFT router, 52.7% token reduction), Thinkless (RL binary token for reasoning). Says it shares RL-for-control philosophy but targets multi-turn verification.\n- **Credit assignment**: Zhang et al. survey, GAE. Uses GAE as baseline.\n\n### Key Results\n- ALFWorld episode-level GRPO: 47.8%±3.1pp (5 seeds). Step-independent h=2: 2%.\n- GAE(λ=1.0): 39% vs episode-level GRPO 38% at step 15 (indistinguishable).\n- B-IAVR: 46% success, 42% verification rate, 52% cost reduction. Pareto-dominates confidence-threshold, random, ARES.\n- ScienceWorld transfer: 3.2× episode/step ratio.\n- 7B scaling: ratio increases from 2.7× to 3.6×.\n- Absolute performance: 48% ALFWorld, far below GAGPO 93.5% and HiPER 97.4%.\n\n### Limitations (stated by authors)\n- Absolute performance 48% far below SOTA (GAGPO 93.5%, HiPER 97.4%).\n- Contribution is diagnostic/methodological, not performance-focused.\n- Selective verification tested on only two environments.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**:\n   - Is the dual mechanism diagnosis genuinely new? Turn-PPO already identified the \"uniform advantage\" problem. What is the incremental contribution?\n   - Is the \"episode-level info matters more than finer credit\" finding surprising or expected?\n   - Is B-IAVR (action-change reward for verification routing) a significant methodological contribution?\n   - How does this compare to Thinkless which also uses RL for binary control tokens?\n\n2. **Related Work Adequacy**:\n   - Are key competitors properly cited and discussed?\n   - Is the positioning honest given the huge performance gap (48% vs 93.5-97.4%)?\n   - Are there missing references?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact**:\n   - Who benefits from this work? Practitioners or researchers?\n   - Is the contrarian \"coarser is better\" finding likely to influence the field?\n   - Is the B-IAVR component likely to be adopted?\n\n4. **Claim Validity vs. Framing**:\n   - Does the paper overclaim? Are the contributions fairly stated?\n   - Is the \"finer credit is unnecessary\" claim too strong given the limited setting (sparse binary rewards)?\n   - The paper says GAGPO/HiPER use \"finer-grained credit assignment and larger models\" — is this a fair characterization?\n\n5. **Weaknesses**: Be specific. Classify as (major) or (minor).\n\n6. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions.\n\n7. **Preliminary Score**: X/6 with justification.\n\nFormat your review as a structured document with headers. Be thorough but fair.","agentId":"a9092ffeee1cf1d4a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough analysis of the paper summary, project files, literature map, paper framing document, previous dream reviews, and competitive landscape, here is my review as Reviewer R2.\n\n---\n\n# R2 Review: Novelty, Positioning, Related Work Coverage, and Significance\n\n## Paper: \"Learning When to Verify: RL-Trained Selective Self-Verification for Multi-Turn Interactive Agents\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n### 1.1 Dual Mechanism Diagnosis (Sign Misattribution + Information Loss)\n\n**Assessment: Moderate novelty, incremental over Turn-PPO.**\n\nTurn-PPO (Li et al., 2512.17008) already identified the \"uniform advantage\" problem in step-independent GRPO: all turns within an episode receive the same advantage regardless of local action quality. The authors claim sign misattribution is a deeper root cause (the advantage *sign* is determined by episode outcome, not local decision quality), with uniform advantage being merely a symptom.\n\nThis distinction has some merit. Turn-PPO describes the *phenomenon* (all advantages are equal), while this paper claims to explain the *mechanism* (why they are equal: group normalization with binary sparse rewards forces sign to track episode outcome). The additional quantification of gamma^24 = 29% return decay for early windows adds specificity. However:\n\n- The information loss mechanism (short context windows cannot represent cross-step strategies) is obvious from first principles. Any practitioner would identify this immediately when seeing h=2 windows.\n- The sign misattribution mechanism follows directly from how GRPO's group normalization interacts with binary rewards. In a failed episode, *all* windows get negative advantage because the trajectory reward is below group mean. This is the expected behavior of the algorithm, not a hidden failure mode.\n- The key intellectual contribution reduces to: \"RAGEN's step-independent implementation, when combined with sparse binary rewards and short context windows, produces degenerate training signal.\" This is a useful engineering observation, not a theoretical discovery.\n\n**Incremental over Turn-PPO:** The paper's own framing document (paper_framing.md) rates Turn-PPO as \"HIGH severity\" attack surface, acknowledging the overlap. The claimed differentiation (symptom vs. root cause, finer vs. coarser solution direction) is real but thin. Both papers diagnose the same fundamental problem; the difference is in characterization depth and solution direction.\n\n### 1.2 \"Episode-Level Credit Suffices\" Finding\n\n**Assessment: Partially expected, partially surprising.**\n\nThe *expected* part: that episode-level training works better than a broken step-independent implementation is unsurprising. Any RL practitioner encountering 0% training success would try full-episode returns as the most basic debugging step.\n\nThe *partially surprising* part: the GAE comparison (GAE lambda=1.0 at 39% vs. episode-level GRPO at 38%, indistinguishable) suggests that sophisticated temporal credit assignment provides no benefit over the simplest possible assignment in this setting. This challenges the implicit assumption behind Turn-PPO, GAGPO, iStar, and HiPER that finer credit always helps.\n\nHowever, this finding is heavily qualified:\n- It holds only for sparse binary rewards (ALFWorld success/failure). With denser reward signals, finer credit assignment would almost certainly help.\n- GAGPO achieves 93.5% on the same benchmark (vs. 48% here), suggesting finer credit *does* help when properly implemented. The paper's claim that \"coarser suffices\" is therefore misleading: coarser suffices *relative to broken finer*, not relative to *correct* finer.\n- The GAE(lambda=1.0) result effectively proves that episode-level GRPO *is* GAE at the boundary, not that it is *better* than GAE. This undermines the \"contrarian\" framing.\n\n### 1.3 B-IAVR (Action-Change Reward for Verification Routing)\n\n**Assessment: Moderate novelty, straightforward application of existing patterns.**\n\nThe idea of using action-change (whether verification changes the agent's subsequent action) as an intrinsic reward for learning when to verify is clean and well-motivated. The insight that \"verification is only valuable when it changes behavior\" is practical and avoids oracle labels.\n\nHowever:\n- Architecturally, B-IAVR is Thinkless (Fang et al., NeurIPS 2025) applied to verification routing in multi-turn agents. Thinkless uses an RL-trained binary control token to decide when to invoke extended thinking. B-IAVR uses an RL-trained binary decision to invoke verification. The structural isomorphism is clear.\n- The reward signal (action-change) is the primary differentiator from Thinkless (which uses task accuracy) and ARES (which uses token efficiency). This is a reasonable design choice but not a conceptual leap.\n- The Lagrangian cost constraint for target verification rate is standard from constrained MDPs (Altman, 1999). Not novel.\n- The results (46% success, 42% verification rate, 52% cost reduction) show a reasonable cost-performance tradeoff, but the absolute performance is too low (46% vs. 93-97% SOTA) to make the cost reduction practically meaningful. Who would deploy a 46% success agent with 52% less verification when 93% agents exist?\n\n### 1.4 Comparison to Thinkless\n\nThinkless trains an RL-based binary control token for reasoning in single-turn settings. B-IAVR trains an RL-based binary decision for verification in multi-turn settings. The differences:\n\n| Dimension | Thinkless | B-IAVR |\n|-----------|-----------|--------|\n| Domain | Single-turn reasoning | Multi-turn agent |\n| Decision | Think/no-think | Verify/no-verify |\n| Reward | Task accuracy + token penalty | Action-change + Lagrangian cost |\n| Training | Two-stage (SFT then RL) | Single-stage RL |\n\nThese are non-trivial adaptations (multi-turn complicates credit assignment for the routing decision itself; action-change is a self-supervised signal). But the conceptual contribution beyond \"Thinkless for agents\" is limited.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Related Work Adequacy\n\n### 2.1 Coverage Assessment\n\nThe paper covers four areas (Multi-turn RL, Self-verification, Adaptive compute, Credit assignment). Based on the literature map (which identifies 50+ relevant papers), the coverage has significant gaps:\n\n**Critical omissions:**\n- **Turn-PPO (Li et al., 2512.17008):** The single most directly related work. Identifies \"uniform advantage\" problem in step-independent GRPO and proposes turn-level MDP + value function. The paper's sign misattribution diagnosis is directly responsive to Turn-PPO's characterization, yet Turn-PPO is not properly engaged with. Mentioning it in passing is insufficient; the paper must explain what it adds beyond Turn-PPO's diagnosis.\n- **GiGPO (Feng et al., NeurIPS 2025):** Achieves 86.1% on ALFWorld with nested group credit assignment (same benchmark, same model class). A direct competitor showing that proper fine-grained credit dramatically outperforms episode-level. Its absence from experimental comparison is a critical gap.\n- **PA-MoE (Li et al., 2026):** 93.8% ALFWorld with 1.5B model. Smaller model, vastly better performance. Not cited.\n- **HCAPO (Tan et al., 2026):** +13.8% on ALFWorld over GRPO via hindsight credit. Directly contradicts \"coarser is better.\"\n- **Process Reward Models (Lightman et al., 2023; Math-Shepherd):** Step-level credit assignment literature entirely absent despite being the dominant paradigm for credit assignment in LLM training.\n\n**Important omissions:**\n- **Derailer-Rerailer (Wan et al., ACL 2025 Findings):** Binary verification trigger for reasoning chains. Most direct competitor in selective verification.\n- **Agent-Omit (Ning et al., ICML 2026):** RL-trained adaptive omission. Same RL-for-cost-efficiency approach, opposite direction (subtract vs. add).\n- **Self-Verification Dilemma (Long et al., 2026):** Empirically validates >90% verification is confirmatory. Directly supports B-IAVR's motivation but not cited.\n- **ArCHer (Zhou et al., ICML 2024):** Foundational hierarchical multi-turn RL framework. Establishes that multi-turn RL needs structure beyond flat approaches.\n\n### 2.2 Positioning Honesty\n\n**The performance gap is inadequately addressed.**\n\nThe paper achieves 48% on ALFWorld. Contemporary work achieves:\n- Self-Guide: 72.7% (same model, Qwen-1.7B)\n- GiGPO: 86.1% (Qwen2.5-1.5B, smaller)\n- GAGPO: 93.5% (Qwen2.5-1.5B)\n- PA-MoE+GiGPO: 93.8% (Qwen2.5-1.5B)\n- HiPER: 97.4% (Qwen2.5-7B)\n- ReflAct: 93.3% (prompting GPT-4)\n\nThe paper states its contribution is \"diagnostic/methodological, not performance-focused.\" This is an acceptable framing *if* properly positioned. However, claiming that GAGPO/HiPER use \"finer-grained credit assignment and larger models\" is *partially misleading*:\n- GAGPO achieves 93.5% with **1.5B** (same or smaller than this paper's 1.7B)\n- PA-MoE achieves 93.8% with **1.5B**\n- Self-Guide achieves 72.7% with **the same 1.7B model**\n\nThe \"larger models\" characterization applies only to HiPER's 7B result. For the 1.5B results (GAGPO, PA-MoE), the performance gap cannot be attributed to model size. The paper should honestly state: \"Methods with proper credit assignment achieve 86-94% on the same benchmark with similar model sizes. Our episode-level approach achieves 48%, demonstrating that correct-but-uniform credit is far from optimal. Our contribution is explaining *why* the broken default fails, not proposing a competitive solution.\"\n\n### 2.3 Missing References Summary\n\nThe paper needs at minimum 15-20 additional references covering:\n1. Classic credit assignment (GAE, REINFORCE, reward shaping Ng 1999, HER)\n2. Concurrent multi-turn agent RL (Turn-PPO, GiGPO, GAGPO, HCAPO, PA-MoE, ArCHer)\n3. Selective verification competitors (Derailer-Rerailer, Agent-Omit, Sherlock)\n4. Self-correction foundations (Kamoi survey, Long et al. 2026, Reflexion)\n5. Process reward models (Lightman 2023, Math-Shepherd, AgentPRM)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Significance and Impact\n\n### 3.1 Who Benefits?\n\n**Primary audience: Researchers using RAGEN/step-independent frameworks for multi-turn agent training.**\n\nThe diagnostic value is clearest for practitioners who encounter training collapse with step-independent GRPO and need to understand why. The paper provides a clear mechanistic explanation and a simple fix.\n\n**Limited audience for B-IAVR:** The selective verification component is useful only if one's agent achieves reasonable base performance. At 48%, the cost reduction is moot because the agent is not deployment-ready.\n\n### 3.2 Will the \"Coarser is Better\" Finding Influence the Field?\n\n**Unlikely to shift the dominant direction, but may serve as a useful cautionary note.**\n\nThe field has already moved toward finer credit assignment (Turn-PPO, GAGPO, GiGPO, iStar, HiPER) with dramatic performance gains. The paper's finding that episode-level suffices compared to *broken* step-independent does not challenge these methods' effectiveness. GAE(lambda=1.0) matching episode-level GRPO at 39% further shows that the paper's approach is a boundary case of standard methods, not a superior alternative.\n\nThe paper could have more impact if reframed as: \"Before investing in sophisticated credit assignment, ensure your baseline implementation is not fundamentally broken. Step-independent windowing with short context introduces sign errors that no amount of fine-grained credit can fix within the broken framework.\"\n\n### 3.3 Will B-IAVR Be Adopted?\n\n**Low likelihood of direct adoption in current form.**\n\nFor B-IAVR to be adopted, an agent must: (1) already have reasonable performance, (2) use self-verification, and (3) face cost constraints. The paper demonstrates B-IAVR only at 48% base performance, which is not a scenario where practitioners would deploy. For the methods achieving 93-97% (which practitioners would actually deploy), B-IAVR's applicability is undemonstrated.\n\nThe *concept* (RL-trained verification routing with action-change reward) may influence future work if validated at higher absolute performance levels.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Claim Validity vs. Framing\n\n### 4.1 Overclaiming Assessment\n\n**Several instances of overclaiming:**\n\n1. **\"Dual mechanism diagnosis\"** suggests a deep theoretical contribution. In reality: (a) short context loses information (obvious), (b) group normalization with binary rewards produces sign errors in independent windows (follows from algorithm definition). Reframe as \"empirical characterization\" rather than \"diagnosis.\"\n\n2. **\"Episode-level credit, not finer per-token credit, is the key\"** is misleading. GAE matches episode-level (both at ~39%), and GAGPO with proper finer credit achieves 93.5%. The accurate claim is: \"Episode-level credit eliminates sign misattribution introduced by step-independent windowing, and performs comparably to GAE in the sparse-reward setting.\"\n\n3. **\"58% verification cost reduction, only -2pp success loss\"** sounds impressive but: (a) -2pp from 48% to 46% is statistically indistinguishable given reported variance (std ~3pp), (b) 46% absolute performance is not deployment-ready, making cost reduction practically irrelevant.\n\n4. **\"Pareto-dominates confidence-threshold, random, ARES\"** may be technically true but obscures that all methods operate far below competitive performance levels. Dominating weak baselines at low absolute performance is not a strong claim.\n\n### 4.2 \"Finer Credit is Unnecessary\" Claim\n\n**Too strong given the limited setting.**\n\nThis claim holds only when:\n- Rewards are sparse and binary (success/failure)\n- The comparison is against broken step-independent (not against correct finer methods like GAGPO)\n- Absolute performance is not the objective\n\nIn any setting with denser rewards, longer horizons with meaningful intermediate outcomes, or performance targets above 50%, finer credit assignment demonstrably helps (GAGPO 93.5%, HiPER 97.4%). The paper should explicitly scope this finding: \"Under sparse binary rewards, episode-level credit eliminates sign misattribution without requiring finer-grained methods. This does not generalize to settings where intermediate reward signals are available.\"\n\n### 4.3 Characterization of GAGPO/HiPER\n\n**Partially unfair.**\n\nSaying they use \"finer-grained credit assignment and larger models\" is inaccurate for GAGPO (93.5% with 1.5B, same or smaller model). GAGPO uses grouped value proxy (no learned critic) with step-aligned advantages. It is critic-free and does not use larger models. The fair characterization is: \"GAGPO and similar methods achieve much higher performance by implementing correct finer-grained credit within the same model class. Our contribution is orthogonal: we diagnose why the *broken* coarse default fails, not compete with correct finer alternatives.\"\n\n---\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**(M1) The core diagnostic contribution is incremental over Turn-PPO.** Turn-PPO already identifies the uniform advantage problem in step-independent training. The additional formalization (sign misattribution + gamma decay quantification) adds precision but not a qualitatively new insight. The contrarian solution direction (coarser vs. finer) is the main differentiation, but GAE matching episode-level undermines the \"coarser is *better*\" claim; it is merely \"coarser is *equivalent* in this boundary case.\"\n\n**(M2) Episode-level GRPO is not a novel algorithm.** It is REINFORCE with trajectory returns within the GRPO framework. The paper correctly acknowledges this, but it means the \"fix\" has zero algorithmic novelty. If the fix is a one-line configuration change (set context_window_mode=full), the contribution reduces to \"we identified a bug in a specific framework's default settings.\"\n\n**(M3) Absolute performance gap destroys practical relevance.** 48% vs. 93-97% on the same benchmark with similar models means neither the training methodology nor the selective verification is competitive. The paper cannot claim practical value for the cost reduction (who optimizes cost for a 48% agent?) and cannot claim the training methodology is sufficient (93% agents exist).\n\n**(M4) B-IAVR results are not statistically separable from baselines.** 46% vs. 44.3% (confidence threshold) is +1.7pp, within the ~3pp seed variance. The Pareto dominance claim requires formal statistical testing that is not provided. Without significance tests, \"dominates\" is unsupported.\n\n**(M5) Related work is critically inadequate.** Missing Turn-PPO (most direct competitor), GiGPO/GAGPO (showing finer credit works much better), PA-MoE (93.8% with smaller model), process reward model literature, and selective verification competitors. The paper cannot be properly evaluated without positioning against these works.\n\n### Minor\n\n**(m1) Two environments (ALFWorld + ScienceWorld) are both text-based discrete-action domains.** Generality claims require at least one continuous/web/API-calling environment (WebShop, WebArena, ToolBench).\n\n**(m2) The \"3.2x episode/step ratio\" for ScienceWorld transfer is a single number without variance.** Likely single-seed. Cannot support generalization claims.\n\n**(m3) 7B scaling result (ratio increases from 2.7x to 3.6x) is reported without explaining the mechanism.** Why would larger models suffer more from sign misattribution? A hypothesis (larger models are more susceptible to spurious gradient signals?) should be offered.\n\n**(m4) The paper conflates \"step-independent\" (RAGEN's specific implementation) with \"fine-grained credit\" (the general principle).** Discrediting RAGEN's implementation does not discredit the principle of finer credit assignment.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **How does your sign misattribution formalization differ from Turn-PPO's \"uniform advantage\" characterization beyond being a restatement in more precise terms?** Turn-PPO identifies that all turns get equal advantage; you identify that this advantage's sign tracks episode outcome. Is the latter not simply the mechanism underlying the former? What does your formalization predict that Turn-PPO's does not?\n\n2. **GAE(lambda=1.0) matches episode-level GRPO at 39% vs. 38%. Since GAE(lambda=1.0) with gamma=1 is mathematically equivalent to episode-level returns, does this not prove that your \"episode-level GRPO\" is simply a specific case of standard GAE, rather than a contrarian alternative to fine-grained credit?**\n\n3. **GAGPO achieves 93.5% on ALFWorld with Qwen2.5-1.5B (same or smaller model). How do you reconcile your claim that \"episode-level credit suffices\" with the demonstrated 45pp improvement from proper fine-grained credit (GAGPO) on the same benchmark?** Is the correct interpretation not: \"episode-level is better than *broken* step-independent, but far worse than *correct* fine-grained\"?\n\n4. **The B-IAVR success rate (46%) minus always-on (48%) is -2pp with std ~3pp. Can you provide a paired t-test or bootstrap confidence interval demonstrating this difference is statistically distinguishable from zero?** If not, the \"negligible loss\" claim is unsupported.\n\n5. **What prevents a practitioner from simply setting h=T (full context) in RAGEN's configuration to achieve episode-level training?** If this is a one-line fix, how do you justify the contribution as more than \"documenting a framework default that should be changed\"?\n\n---\n\n## 7. Preliminary Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper addresses a real issue (step-independent RL failing for multi-turn agents) and provides useful empirical characterization (h-sweep, sign correlation analysis, GAE comparison). The B-IAVR concept (action-change reward for verification routing) is clean and practical.\n\nHowever, the paper falls below the acceptance threshold for a top venue because:\n\n1. **Novelty is insufficient.** The diagnostic contribution is incremental over Turn-PPO. The fix (episode-level) is textbook REINFORCE. B-IAVR is Thinkless applied to verification. No component clears the novelty bar independently, and their combination does not produce emergent novelty.\n\n2. **The \"contrarian\" framing is undermined by the paper's own evidence.** GAE matching episode-level proves they are equivalent, not that coarser is better. GAGPO at 93.5% proves finer credit *is* better when correctly implemented. The actual finding is \"broken finer is worse than correct coarser,\" which is tautological.\n\n3. **Practical relevance is destroyed by the performance gap.** 48% vs. 93-97% means neither the training methodology nor the cost reduction is useful in practice. The contribution is purely academic/diagnostic, but the paper frames it as practically impactful.\n\n4. **Related work coverage is critically inadequate.** Missing the most directly relevant competitor (Turn-PPO), the strongest evidence against the paper's claims (GAGPO, PA-MoE), and entire categories of relevant literature.\n\n5. **Statistical rigor for B-IAVR claims is insufficient.** Key comparisons are within noise bounds without significance tests.\n\nFor acceptance, the paper would need: (a) explicit engagement with Turn-PPO showing clear differentiation, (b) comparison against GAGPO/GiGPO as \"correct fine-grained\" baselines, (c) honest repositioning as a diagnostic contribution without \"contrarian\" overclaiming, (d) substantially expanded related work, and (e) significance testing for B-IAVR comparisons.\n\n---\n\nThis is my complete review as R2. The paper has genuine merit as a diagnostic/engineering contribution but overclaims its novelty and practical significance relative to the competitive landscape. The gap between 48% and 93-97% SOTA on the same benchmark is the single most damaging fact that the paper must directly confront rather than deflect."}],"totalDurationMs":230896,"totalTokens":73637,"totalToolUseCount":29,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":67608,"output_tokens":5526,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":500},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":5526,"cache_read_input_tokens":67608,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":500,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":9,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014Csk7ro9P85K6e6fhcC7is","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to run the roundtable process:\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done)\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing (R1 sees R2's review and vice versa)\n- Step 3: Convergence (identify agreements and disagreements)\n- Step 4: Synthesis (final consolidated report)\n\nLet me now synthesize the two reviews into a roundtable discussion and produce the final report.\n\nKey findings from each reviewer:\n\n**R1 (Method + Experiments): 4/6 Weak Accept**\n- Strengths: well-structured diagnosis, good empirical coverage, clean B-IAVR design\n\n\n- Major weaknesses: dual mechanism not cleanly separated, absolute performance gap, -2pp B-IAVR not significant, single environment\n- Minor: GAE comparison incomplete (only lambda=1.0), causal intervention selection bias, RAGEN-specific scope\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work): 3/6 Weak Reject**\n- Strengths: addresses a real issue, useful empirical characterization, clean B-IAVR concept\n- Major weaknesses: incremental over Turn-PPO, episode-level GRPO is not novel (it's REINFORCE), absolute performance gap destroys practical relevance, B-IAVR not statistically significant\n- Related work is critically inadequate—missing detailed Turn-PPO engagement, GiGPO, PA-MoE, and process reward models\n- The \"contrarian\" framing falls apart when you look at the evidence: GAE matching actually suggests equivalence rather than superiority, and GAGPO hitting 93.5% shows that finer credit assignment does work when implemented correctly\n\nNow synthesizing across both reviews, there's clear consensus on several fronts: the dual mechanism observation is real but has structural issues, the 48% versus 93.5%+ performance gap is genuinely problematic, the -2pp B-IAVR difference lacks statistical significance, and the GAE comparison is intriguing but too narrow. Both reviewers also appreciate the experimental scope. The key divergence is the overall assessment—one leans toward weak accept while the other toward weak reject.\n\nThe crux comes down to whether the diagnostic value justifies publication despite the novelty concerns and framing problems. Turn-PPO overlap is a legitimate worry, and the fact that GAGPO's superior results directly contradict the paper's central claim is hard to overlook. The diagnostic contribution sits right at the boundary of marginal. Weighing everything, I'm settling on a weak accept with substantial revisions required, though it's genuinely borderline. The diagnostic insight has merit, but the related work gaps and misleading framing need serious attention.\n\nThe paper's real strengths are the phase transition behavior in the hyperparameter sweep, the empirical validation of sign misattribution, and the action-change reward formulation for B-IAVR. These are solid contributions, though the overclaiming and missing related work undermine the overall impact. Time to synthesize this into the final report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11555,"cache_read_input_tokens":68826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11555},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"865e8d8a-d167-43f7-87ae-b7ba5c42080f","uuid":"a7efa0af-d69a-42e7-9668-b0bf46c965f1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014Csk7ro9P85K6e6fhcC7is","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers have completed their independent assessments. Let me now run the roundtable synthesis: cross-share findings, identify convergence points, and produce the final report.\n\n**Roundtable Summary:**\n- R1 (Method+Experiments): 4/6 Weak Accept\n- R2 (Novelty+Related Work): 3/6 Weak Reject\n\nKey convergence points: both agree the diagnostic contribution is real but has framing issues; both flag the massive performance gap (48% vs 93.5%+) and the statistical non-significance of B-IAVR's -2pp claim. R2 raises critical concerns about novelty overlap with Turn-PPO and missing related work that R1 implicitly supports.\n\nLet me now produce and submit the final consolidated review report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11555,"cache_read_input_tokens":68826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11555},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"865e8d8a-d167-43f7-87ae-b7ba5c42080f","uuid":"184cb7d6-d1e7-4f20-936e-779767986a61"}
